      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess the ability of imaging coronary arteries noninvasively with magnetic resonance angiography technique (MRCA) and its diagnostic value in coronary artery disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "MRCA was performed on 33 subjects, including 12 healthy adult volunteers and 21 patients with available recent X-ray contrast angiography (XCA) results. MRCA technique consisted of a gradient echo sequence (Turbo-Cine), two-dimensional acquisition, fat suppression, peripheral gating. Subjects were imaged in supine position using a standard body coil, without breath-hold."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "All 33 subjects were imaged successfully. Identification of the coronary artery was possible for the right coronary, left main stem, left anterior, and left circumflex arteries respectively in 97%, 100%, 91% and 91% with a visualized mean length of 51 +/- 15 (28-80 mm), 11 +/- 4 (4-22 mm), 44 +/- 13 (24-66 mm), 40 +/- 12 (20-66 mm). In the 21 subjects with available XCA results, 78% (29/37) proximal stenoses of the coronary arteries were detected with MRCA."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "At current stage, MRCA can depict most of the hemodynamically significant proximal stenoses but not the distals. This procedure can be used as an alternative imaging application under certain clinical indications."
        }
      ]},
